{
  "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
  "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: JEFFREY K. HAYNES, ESQ., Bloomfield Hills, Michigan.; on behalf of the Petitioner. MALCOLM L. STEWART, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Jeffrey K. Haynes)",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The plain English reading of Section 2501 of Title 28, its phrasing compared to the jurisdictional grants to the Court of Federal Claims, the contemporaneous legal history of its predecessor, and this Court's decisions in Irwin and Franconia Associates compel the conclusion that Section 2501 does not limit subject matter jurisdiction and should be applied to the government as an ordinary waivable affirmative defense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0011",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The plain text of Section 2501, which reads \"Every claim of which the United States Court of Federal Claims has jurisdiction shall be barred unless the petition thereon is filed within 6 years\" after it first accrues, assumes subject matter jurisdiction, and if it assumes subject matter jurisdiction it cannot logically limit subject matter jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Did the prior statutes have a different structure?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: It didn't say that. You're saying that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: But you can -- was Congress aware of that general rule when it last revised the statute?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0000-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Haynes, isn't it less radical and, indeed, more in accord with the language of Irwin to -- to say that what Irwin overruled was not the whole principle that this statute of limitation is -- is -- and others that relate to sovereign immunity, is jurisdictional, but rather the much more limited rule that -- that statutes of limitations which are jurisdictional are not subject to equitable tolling?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Haynes",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HAYNES: Yes, Mr. Chief Justice, that's correct. That's what Franconia ruled. However, Franconia reiterated the Irwin rule, which is that once sovereign immunity is waived the statute of limitations applies to the government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, that -- that assumes that the government has waived in every case. If it hasn't waived, I have to do it anyway."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stewart",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: I think there are two bases on which we would distinguish that language. The first is by its terms Chief Justice Rehnquist's sentence was addressed to equitable tolling, not to waivibility."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "189d4ae6e2c5370fcbeaade2d5e37ca6068a224e95b144527949e24c1df21ebf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-189d4ae6e2c5-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HAYNES: With respect, I suggest that the government won the battle, but lost the war on Irwin."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1164",
      "confidence": 0.0,
